Acronym for *Distinguished Encoding Rules*. It is a strict subset of the ASN.1 encoding rules defined in the [ITU-T X.690, 2002](https://www.itu.int/ITU-T/studygroups/com17/languages/X.690-0207.pdf) specification and used to encode any type of data into a binary sequence. DER is mostly used in specific fields, such as cryptography, where data must be encoded in a standard and predictable way.

On Bitcoin, ECDSA signatures are encoded in DER format. They consist of two encoded numbers (`r`,`s`). The signature format consists of the following elements:

```text
0x30 | length |  0x02 | r-length | r | 0x02 | s-length | s
```

With:
* `0x30` (1 byte): identifier of a compound structure;
* `length` (1 byte): length of the following data;
* `0x02` (1 byte): data type identifier `INTEGER` (integer);
* `r-length` (1 byte): length of the following `r` value (generally 32 or 33 bytes);
* `r` (variable): `r` value as a big-endian integer (*big-endian*);
* `0x02` (1 byte): data type identifier `INTEGER` (integer);
* `s-length` (1 byte): length of the following `s` value (generally 32 or 33 bytes);
* `s` (variable): `s` value as a big-endian integer (*big-endian*).

In a Bitcoin transaction, a byte is added at the end of a DER signature to indicate the type of SigHash used.

Refers to the process of generating child key pairs from a parent key pair (private key and public key) and a chain code within a deterministic and hierarchical (HD) wallet. This process makes it possible to segment branches and organize a wallet into different levels with many child key pairs, all of which can be recovered knowing only the basic recovery information (the mnemonic phrase and the optional passphrase). To derive a child key, the `HMAC-SHA512` function is used with the parent chain code and the concatenation of the parent key and a 32-bit index. There are two types of derivation:
* Normal derivation, which uses the parent public key as the basis of the `HMAC-SHA512` function;
* Hardened derivation, which uses the parent private key as the basis of the `HMAC-SHA512` function;

The result of HMAC-SHA512 is split in two: the first 256 bits are added to the parent key to produce the child key (private, or public after multiplication by the generator point on the elliptic curve), and the remaining 256 bits become the child chain code.

Initial connection points for new Bitcoin nodes joining the network. These seeds, which are in fact specific DNS servers, have their address permanently built into the Bitcoin Core code. When a new node starts, it contacts these servers to obtain a random list of IP addresses of Bitcoin nodes that are presumably active. The new node can thus establish connections with the nodes on this list in order to obtain the information needed to perform its IBD and synchronize on the chain with the most accumulated work. In 2024, here is the list of Bitcoin Core's DNS seeds and the people responsible for maintaining them (https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/master/src/kernel/chainparams.cpp):
* seed.bitcoin.sipa.be: Pieter Wuille;
* dnsseed.bluematt.me: Matt Corallo;
* dnsseed.bitcoin.dashjr-list-of-p2p-nodes.us: Luke Dashjr;
* seed.bitcoin.jonasschnelli.ch: Jonas Schnelli;
* seed.btc.petertodd.net: Peter Todd;
* seed.bitcoin.sprovoost.nl: Sjors Provoost;
* dnsseed.emzy.de: Stephan Oeste;
* seed.bitcoin.wiz.biz: Jason Maurice;
* seed.mainnet.achownodes.xyz: Ava Chow.

DNS seeds are the second means, in order of priority, for a Bitcoin node to establish connections. The first means is to use the `peers.dat` file that the node itself created. This file is naturally empty in the case of a new node, unless the user has modified it manually.

*Note that DNS seeds must not be confused with “seed nodes”, which are the third way of establishing connections.*
